    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d371">

                <pb facs="0478" n="468" sameAs="frus:frus1944v02/pg468"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.796/5–644</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of the British Embassy</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Wright</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Assistant
                        Secretary of State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Berle</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">Ref. 56/187/44</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-05-06">6 May, 1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Berle</hi>: With reference to my letter of
                    May 2nd,<anchor n="6" xml:id="d371-f1"/> I now enclose a copy of a further
                    telegram from Lord Beaverbrook which has just reached us.</p>
                <pb facs="0479" n="469" xml:id="pg469"/>
                <p>For convenience of reference I also enclose herewith a copy of that part of Lord
                    Beaverbrook’s declaration of January 19th,<anchor n="7" xml:id="d371-f2"/> to
                    which he refers in the fourth paragraph of his telegram attached.</p>
                <p>Yours sincerely,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Michael Wright</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>


                <xi:include href="d371-a1.xml" xpointer="d371-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d371-f1" n="6" xml:id="d371-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it was an acknowledgment of Mr. Berle’s letter of April 29, <ref
                        target="#pg462">p. 462</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d371-f2" n="7" xml:id="d371-f2-content">
                <p>For text of this enclosure, see second and third paragraphs of extract from the
                    January 19 speech by Lord Beaverbrook, <ref target="#pg368">pp. 368</ref>–<ref
                        target="#pg369">369</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
